$DIR/main.p:29:2: cannot pass Outer{...} to fmt.Println, which keeps it after returning
    Outer{...} is an interface that points at a copy of its value in f's frame.
    Converting a value larger than one word to an interface puts a copy of the
    value in the frame of the function that converts it (here f, at main.p:29),
    and the interface points at that copy. fmt.Println passes a pointer loaded
    from args[i] to Fprintln (print_linux.p:17), which passes a pointer loaded
    from args[i] to (*printer).doPrint (fmt.p:948), which passes a pointer
    loaded from args[i] to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:915), which passes arg to
    (*printer).fmtBytes (fmt.p:731), which passes ptr to (*printer).printKind
    (value.p:373), which passes ptr to (*printer).printPayload (value.p:233),
    which passes arg to (*printer).handleMethods (value.p:60), which passes arg
    to v.String (which may call main.(F).String) (fmt.p:777), which stores g in
    package variable keep (main.p:16), so the interface would outlive f's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change fmt.Println to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an
    owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:29:2: cannot pass the function literal to fmt.Println, which keeps it after returning
    the function literal is a function literal that uses in. A function literal
    that uses variables of the function around it refers to those variables
    where they live, in f's frame (in is declared at main.p:27). fmt.Println
    passes a pointer reachable from args to Fprintln (print_linux.p:17), which
    passes a pointer reachable from args to (*printer).doPrint (fmt.p:948),
    which passes a pointer reachable from args to (*printer).printArg
    (fmt.p:915), which passes a pointer reachable from arg to
    (*printer).fmtBytes (fmt.p:731), which passes a pointer reachable from ptr
    to (*printer).printKind (value.p:373), which passes a pointer reachable from
    ptr to (*printer).printWord (value.p:209), which passes a pointer reachable
    from word to (*printer).printValue (value.p:76), which passes a pointer
    reachable from ptr to (*printer).handleMethods (value.p:39), which passes a
    pointer reachable from arg to v.String (which may call main.(F).String)
    (fmt.p:777), which stores a pointer reachable from g in package variable
    keep (main.p:16), so the function literal would outlive in.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change fmt.Println to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an
    owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:29:2: cannot pass &in to fmt.Println, which keeps it after returning
    &in points into in. in is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:27).
    fmt.Println passes a pointer reachable from args to Fprintln
    (print_linux.p:17), which passes a pointer reachable from args to
    (*printer).doPrint (fmt.p:948), which passes a pointer reachable from args
    to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:915), which passes a pointer reachable from
    arg to (*printer).fmtBytes (fmt.p:731), which passes a pointer reachable
    from ptr to (*printer).printKind (value.p:373), which passes a pointer
    reachable from ptr to (*printer).printWord (value.p:209), which passes a
    pointer reachable from word to (*printer).printValue (value.p:76), which
    passes a pointer reachable from ptr to (*printer).handleMethods
    (value.p:39), which passes a pointer reachable from arg to v.String (which
    may call main.(F).String) (fmt.p:777), which stores a pointer reachable from
    g in package variable keep (main.p:16), so &in would outlive in.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If fmt.Println should keep its own copy of in, change it to take the value,
    not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[Inner] from new, a
    shared[Inner], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
